IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `comp50-nouns`

-- Row il-50. THE DATE PAIRS AND THE SECTION-NUMBER RUNS, as typed values, and the few functions the tests use.
-- Four defects are recorded, with four controls for the date pairs and two for the saving clause.
-- DEFECT 1 (P1). Regulation 8 of the Companies (Fees) Regulations, 5761-2001, prints the commencement day as "6 Shevat 5761 (1 January 2001)". The Hebrew date computes to 30 January 2001;
--   1 January 2001 is 6 Tevet 5761.
-- DEFECT 2 (P2). Regulation 66 of the Companies (Motion for Compromise or Arrangement) Regulations, 5762-2002, prints the last day of a temporary provision as "12 Tammuz 5768 (15 July 2007)".
--   The Hebrew date computes to 15 July 2008; 15 July 2007 is 29 Tammuz 5767.
-- DEFECT 3 (P3). Section 367(א)(1) of the Companies Law keeps Ordinance sections "164 עד 201, 244 עד 367, 370 עד 382" from the repeal of the Ordinance. The Insolvency and Economic Rehabilitation Law,
--   Sefer HaChukim 2708, section 359 items (4) and (5), cancels the Ordinance's sections 195 to 201 and its chapters 11 to 17.
-- DEFECT 4 (P6). Five signature lines of Kovetz HaTakanot 7639 (the issue printed as 3 April 2016, pp. 946 to 949) print "25 Adar I 5776 (8 March 2016)". The Hebrew date computes to 5 March 2016;
--   8 March 2016 is 28 Adar I 5776.
-- The controls: for each date pair, another pair in the same instrument that agrees; for the saving clause, two other saved Ordinance sections that no cancellation reaches.
-- LIMBS. Limb (a) is the question whether the target is as the citing words say; limb (b) is the question whether the citing words still read so in the latest official text of the citing provision.
-- Each limb says where it was read from (see `Where a reading comes from` in comp50-nouns.l4). The Hebrew Wikisource text is not official.
-- A day for which the sources held give no answer is NOTHING; there is no REFUSE.

§ `Functions of a printed date pair`

GIVEN p IS A `A printed date pair`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the printed pair agrees` MEANS
    p's `the Gregorian day as printed` EQUALS p's `the Gregorian day the Hebrew date computes to`

GIVEN p IS A `A printed date pair`
GIVETH A DATE
`the earlier of the two days` MEANS
    IF p's `the Gregorian day as printed` AT MOST p's `the Gregorian day the Hebrew date computes to`
    THEN p's `the Gregorian day as printed`
    ELSE p's `the Gregorian day the Hebrew date computes to`

GIVEN p IS A `A printed date pair`
GIVETH A DATE
`the later of the two days` MEANS
    IF p's `the Gregorian day as printed` AT LEAST p's `the Gregorian day the Hebrew date computes to`
    THEN p's `the Gregorian day as printed`
    ELSE p's `the Gregorian day the Hebrew date computes to`

GIVEN p IS A `A printed date pair`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days between the two days` MEANS
    DATE_SERIAL (`the later of the two days` p) MINUS DATE_SERIAL (`the earlier of the two days` p)

-- A date that fixes the day a text takes effect. The text is in force from that day on. If the two printed days differ, the days from the earlier one up to (not including) the later one are
-- answered differently by the two halves of the pair, and the text does not say which half governs (fork F1 in NOTES.md), so the answer for those days is NOTHING.
GIVEN p IS A `A printed date pair`
      day IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the regulations are in force on` MEANS
    IF day AT LEAST (`the later of the two days` p)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF day AT LEAST (`the earlier of the two days` p)
         THEN NOTHING
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- A date that fixes the last day a text stays in force ("until the day ..."). A day before both printed days is in force on either half; a day after both is not. Whether "until the day" counts the day itself
-- is a separate question (fork F2 in NOTES.md), so each of the two printed days, and every day between them, is NOTHING.
GIVEN p IS A `A printed date pair`
      day IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the temporary provision is in force on` MEANS
    IF NOT (day AT LEAST (`the earlier of the two days` p))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF NOT (day AT MOST (`the later of the two days` p))
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE NOTHING

§ `Functions of runs of section numbers`

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the larger of` MEANS IF x AT LEAST y THEN x ELSE y

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the smaller of` MEANS IF x AT MOST y THEN x ELSE y

GIVEN a IS A `A run of section numbers`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`how many numbers a run holds` MEANS (a's `last number` MINUS a's `first number`) PLUS 1

-- The numbers two runs have in common: from the larger of the two first numbers to the smaller of the two last numbers, or none.
GIVEN a IS A `A run of section numbers`
      b IS A `A run of section numbers`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`how many numbers two runs share` MEANS
    IF (`the smaller of` (a's `last number`) (b's `last number`)) AT LEAST (`the larger of` (a's `first number`) (b's `first number`))
    THEN ((`the smaller of` (a's `last number`) (b's `last number`)) MINUS (`the larger of` (a's `first number`) (b's `first number`))) PLUS 1
    ELSE 0

